state were hard  to miss such symbolism was rooted in the  very real threat of the German and  Japanese Navy's servicemen and civilians  traveling across the Atlantic during  late 1942 and early 43 faced serious  danger specifically in October and  November of 1942 approximately when  bloom was creating his painting German  subs sank 235 allied military and  merchant ships more than any other  period of the war determining which  tales of nautical tragedy influenced  bloom is difficult though well versed in  literature and current events he rarely  painted seascapes however in the early  1940s bloom created a handful of strange  paintings based on an extended vacation  in Key West these works including Bowie  demonstrate visual similarities to the  raft and color nautical setting and  overall weirdness bloom later recalled  observing buoys on the beach and noted  how these objects used at navigation  markers often exhibited rough textured  surfaces that evoked an underlying  violence during his time at Key West  bloom also observed military servicemen  and may have seen naval training  involving life rafts off  Beach such as that depicted in this  photograph from the era while castaways  appeared throughout wartime visual  culture numerous details in the raft are  aligned with one specific and dramatic  survival story involving three US  servicemen the story began on January  sixteenth 1942 less than two months  after the attack on Pearl Harbor when a  small Navy aircraft crashed in the South  Pacific the pilot Harold Dixon a 42 year  old aviator miscalculated his  coordinates and landed into what he  assumed were unfriendly waters as the  bombers sank quickly Dixon and the two  young enlisted men in his crew Jean  aldric and Anthony pastilla managed to  secure only a few supplies during their  subsequent voyage they traveled more  than 1,000 miles in a small rubber rat  along the way these three men battled  sharks violent storms an unrelenting Sun  and emotional and physical trauma they  survived for 34 days by consuming  rainwater and small fish before reaching  an island in the South Pacific where  indigenous Islanders provided aid and  contacted us authorities severely  malnourished and near death the trio  eventually arrived at Pearl Harbor in  February 1942 this photograph taken  during the recovery shows each man  standing with the aid of a cane while  their raft rest behind them ensuing  media coverage celebrated Dixon and his  crew as patriotic heroes who exemplified  the can-do spirit necessary for American  victory abroad Life magazine published  an account of their survivors tale  written by dixon and illustrated with  photographs of the of the men after  their subsequent recovery rescue  recovery and ceremonial ceremonial  citations awarded by Admiral Chester  Nimitz this story gained an even wider  audience in the spring of nineteen  forty-two with the publication of a  novel titled the raft written by Robert  Trumbull Honolulu correspondent for the  New York Times the RAF told the tale  from Dixon's perspective an instant hit  the novel received rave reviews was  named a book of the month club selection  and contributed significantly to the  trio's heroic reputation politicians  praised the men as well including vice  president henry wallace who characters  characterization of their survival  appear  on the cover of the first edition quote  the story that generations of Americans  will be telling their children to  illustrate man's ability to master any  fate in quote Wallace's quote is one of  the many links between blooms painting  and this epic tale the vice president's  words were part of a well-known speech  distributed by the OWI to artists  enlisted to submit poster designs in  1942 the books first edition also  featured seven photographs including a  view of the three recovered sailors in  uniform and chatting with naval officers  while sitting in the raft they shared  this image also distributed widely in  the international press bears a striking  resemblance to the shape and perspective  of blooms raft other similarities  include a cylinder of carbon dioxide  used to inflate the raft that appears in  each image at the far left additionally  Dickson recalled how he recorded the  number of days spent at sea with a  series of hash marks made on the side of  the raft that appeared in photographs  featured in the book accordingly blooms  raft included a series of similar marks  as well as the starburst shaped design  on the vessels outer edge finally though  the men in blooms painting appear oddly  similar the artists demonstrated his  awareness of dixon in a pencil sketch  this untitled work dated 1942 shows a  strong similarity to Dixon's widely  distributed photograph and forgive me I  don't have an image of that but believe  me it's him it's definitely Dickson has  a very similar gaunt face government  records show that bloom submitted his  painting to the OWI in the fall of  nineteen forty two in order to be  transferred into a poster and  distributed widely however by November  the project was suppressed or to use  agency terminology killed at this time  OWI officials had begun moving away from  traumatic unsettling mg imagery seen in  the work of bloom and Ben Sean and  instead encouraging content similar to  rockwells more family-friendly fair the  decision to trade Shaun's social realism  and blooms magic realism for a more  comforting commercial realism was part  of a complex political process in short  the OWI slowly replaced  original staffers mostly new deal  loyalists on the political left such as  Sean with business friendly advertising  professionals who saw little reason to  display the wars violence after all  public opinion polls suggested Americans  had little appetite for visual reminders  of the horrors their loved ones faced  fighting abroad and instead favoured  posters with more pleasant imagery  though rejected for official use blooms  painted blooms painting demonstrated a  complicated negotiation between timeless  tragedies and a contemporary and  contemporary fears of a global war  against fascism the scene is  unquestionably intense these three  cadaverous bodies covered tightly by  intensely red singed skin suggests  anatomy's beginning to bear little  resemblance to human forms their gaunt  frames are juxtaposed jarringly against  the bright yellow raft which floats  strangely on top of and not within the  oceans unnaturally flat and calm bluish  green surface the final effect echoes a  critics description of an earlier work  by bloom quote it says morbidly  realistic as a whiff of a putra sent  corpse the OWI s decision to trade one  form of realism for another forced  modern artists such as bloom to seek  other options for supporting the war  less than two years after the owi dumped  the raft bloom completed fracture ward  commissioned by Abbott Laboratories a  chicago-based pharmaceutical corporation  fracture Ward depicts wounded soldiers  recovering stateside the canvas is  particularly memorable for the elaborate  system of traction equipment that frames  the patients on the far right though  both images deal with pain the figures  on the left are well cared for and  clearly mending specifically in a modern  hospital moreover unlike the raft  fracture Ward demonstrates blooms return  to drawing upon his own experiences in  fact while preparing for fracture ward  the artist observed servicemen  recovering at halloran General in Staten  Island the finished canvas was  reproduced in Abbott's promotional  publications and included in traveling  exhibitions that featured other war  themed paintings and as a side note I'll  just point out that the figure on the  right is involved in some sort of arts  and  crafts activity similar to the previous  discussion mentioned thus while blooms  OWI proposal was rejected corporate  sponsors proved willing collaborators  other artists faced a similar situation  and in fact Abbott Laboratories was one  of the most important private  co-sponsors of war art Abbott's most  important project was arguably Thomas  Hart Benton swell known series the year  of peril painted in 1942 Benton's  canvases including starry night are  filled with violence suffering and with  the art historic an art historian Erica  das has called pseudo surrealism  although intended for government  distribution officials labeled the  series too intense despite criticism  from government officials and art  critics that such unrestrained violence  was counterproductive to the war effort  Benton series was seen widely in  newsreels and in a lavish full-color  brochure sponsored by Abbott and that's  the cover of that brochure on the Left  determining why government officials  would accept one image and reject  another involves multiple factors in  closing it seems more important to note  simply that blooms scene of nautical  suffering was one example of many  created during the war and that this and  similar images projected an unsettling  strange visual experience that offered  viewers an alternative form of realism  than the photographic horrors of war  seen in newspapers magazines and  newsreels the appeal of a month more  unorthodox unpredictable system of magic  realism during this era is in many ways  not surprising after all wartime  anxieties touched countless domestic  economic and cultural standards as  millions of men enlisted and shipped out  overseas preconceived notions regarding  the rules of women and minorities in  public and private life were challenged  upset and reworked these resulting  shifts many of which were sudden and  extreme likely let it likely led many  Americans to see parallels between  wartime culture and the visual  instability of blooms grotesque realism  the suppression of blooms painting  reveals the politicized bureaucracy that  oversaw US government propaganda during  World  or two in fact blooms work was only one  of several proposals by well-known  modern artists rejected by officials who  sought to mitigate representations of  the wars harsh realities blooms attempt  to negotiate this political minefield by  combining classical imagery with  contemporary tales of nautical suffering  met an unfortunate end yet with the raft  bloom formed a scene with a powerful  cultural resonance that few other  paintings from the era suppressed or not  could claim thank you
 

